meta {
  name: View the roster
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{groupService}}/roster?group={{groupKey}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  group: {{groupKey}}
}

tests {
  test("names the members and who holds each live connection", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.members).to.be.an("array");
    expect(res.body.sessions).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  # View the roster

  The group's roster beside the connections the relay is carrying for it, each with its holder named.

  `member` on a connection is the name whose id that connection's token subject matches, and empty where the subject derives from no member of this group.
  An empty one is what a stranger looks like between the sweep that finds them and the kick that closes them.

  `stream` is the name inside the group, which is the name `List group streams` answers under, so the two join without deriving the prefix rule again.
  The pair answer different questions of the same relay and neither holds a copy: the index is what a member may open, and this is who is connected.

  Read through on every call: what is live comes off the relay and is never this service's memory.

  The key rides in the query here rather than in a body, the way the stream index takes it, a GET having no body a cache or a proxy will honour.
}
